The weak TLS init function again, in ANGLE this time.

M155's patch 0033 found this in V8 and CLAUDE.md records it as the sharpest
thing that milestone found. It is the same bug, in a different library,
reached for the first time because M165 is the first program here that links
ANGLE's GL entry points.

  ld.lld: error: relocation R_X86_64_PLT32 out of range: -549852782059 is not
  in [-2147483648, 2147483647]; references 'thread-local initialization
  routine for gl::gCurrentValidContext'

-549852782059 is very close to -512 GiB, which is where this OS loads
programs, and the target is therefore at zero.

Context.h declares

  extern thread_local Context *gCurrentValidContext;

and Context.cpp defines it as nullptr. A declaration with no initializer in
sight cannot be known to be constant-initialized, so every translation unit
that READS the variable emits a reference to its TLS init function - and the
defining translation unit emits no such function, because the variable IS
constant-initialized. The reference resolves to absolute zero, which a
PC-relative branch reaches only from an image near zero. On Linux it always
is.

What made this one loud where V8's was a single symbol is where the readers
are: GetValidGlobalContext() is ANGLE_INLINE in global_state.h and every GL
entry point in entry_points_gles_*_autogen.cpp calls it, so the failure
arrives as hundreds of identical errors from generated files.

egl::gCurrentThread in global_state.h has exactly the same shape - extern
thread_local in a header, = nullptr in the source, read through an inline
function in that header - and is fixed here rather than left to be found by
the next link.

constinit says what was already true, in the one place every reader can see
it, and no init function is referenced at all. It is also what upstream would
want: the variables are nullptr-initialized and the keyword documents it.

diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.cpp b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.cpp
index 554cd5b..0be6e1e 100644
--- a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.cpp
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.cpp
@@ -659,7 +659,7 @@ void SetCurrentValidContextTLS(Context *context)
     gCurrentValidContext = context;
 }
 #else
-thread_local Context *gCurrentValidContext = nullptr;
+constinit thread_local Context *gCurrentValidContext = nullptr;
 #endif
 
 // Handle setting the current context in TLS on different platforms
diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.h b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.h
index 01ff2d7..ebf8cc6 100644
--- a/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.h
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libANGLE/Context.h
@@ -1207,7 +1207,20 @@ class [[nodiscard]] ScopedContextRef
 extern Context *GetCurrentValidContextTLS();
 extern void SetCurrentValidContextTLS(Context *context);
 #else
-extern thread_local Context *gCurrentValidContext;
+// constinit, and not for tidiness. Every translation unit that merely READS
+// this variable emits a reference to its "TLS init function", because a
+// declaration with no initializer in sight cannot be known to be
+// constant-initialized - and the defining translation unit emits no such
+// function, because it IS. The reference is then resolved to absolute zero,
+// and a PC-relative branch from the image to zero only fits in 32 bits if the
+// image is near it. On Linux it always is. On a platform that loads programs
+// at 512 GiB it is not, and the link fails with R_X86_64_PLT32 out of range
+// against a function nobody calls - here, from every GL entry point in
+// entry_points_gles_*_autogen.cpp at once.
+//
+// constinit says the thing that is already true, in the one place every reader
+// can see it.
+extern constinit thread_local Context *gCurrentValidContext;
 #endif
 
 extern void SetCurrentValidContext(Context *context);
diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/libGLESv2/global_state.cpp b/third_party/angle/src/libGLESv2/global_state.cpp
index a6644a4..91f323d 100644
--- a/third_party/angle/src/libGLESv2/global_state.cpp
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libGLESv2/global_state.cpp
@@ -123,7 +123,7 @@ void SetCurrentThreadTLS(Thread *thread)
     gCurrentThread = thread;
 }
 #else
-thread_local Thread *gCurrentThread = nullptr;
+constinit thread_local Thread *gCurrentThread = nullptr;
 #endif
 
 gl::Context *GetGlobalLastContext()
diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/libGLESv2/global_state.h b/third_party/angle/src/libGLESv2/global_state.h
index 7810ce4..c2538b0 100644
--- a/third_party/angle/src/libGLESv2/global_state.h
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/libGLESv2/global_state.h
@@ -36,7 +36,20 @@ class Thread;
 extern Thread *GetCurrentThreadTLS();
 extern void SetCurrentThreadTLS(Thread *thread);
 #else
-extern thread_local Thread *gCurrentThread;
+// constinit, and not for tidiness. Every translation unit that merely READS
+// this variable emits a reference to its "TLS init function", because a
+// declaration with no initializer in sight cannot be known to be
+// constant-initialized - and the defining translation unit emits no such
+// function, because it IS. The reference is then resolved to absolute zero,
+// and a PC-relative branch from the image to zero only fits in 32 bits if the
+// image is near it. On Linux it always is. On a platform that loads programs
+// at 512 GiB it is not, and the link fails with R_X86_64_PLT32 out of range
+// against a function nobody calls - here, from every GL entry point in
+// entry_points_gles_*_autogen.cpp at once.
+//
+// constinit says the thing that is already true, in the one place every reader
+// can see it.
+extern constinit thread_local Thread *gCurrentThread;
 #endif
 
 gl::Context *GetGlobalLastContext();
